The new engine now does four things the simple sweep could not: it learns from trial metadata, predicts the response surface, estimates uncertainty/novelty, and chooses future experiments with an acquisition function rather than pure random search.

The core decision rule is:

A(E)=
\hat Y(E)
+\beta\,U(E)
+\gamma\,N(E)
-\lambda_C C(E)
-\lambda_R R(E)

where \hat Y is predicted performance, U is uncertainty, N is novelty, and cost/risk can be penalized. Early in the run, \beta is high, so the system explores. Later it anneals downward and exploits what it has learned.

That gives your idea a clean computational interpretation:

\boxed{\text{Randomness + metadata + prediction = guided future search}}

Randomness supplies candidate futures. Metadata tells us what happened in previous futures. The surrogate model estimates what likely happens next. AI—or any sufficiently capable model—can then enrich those vectors with semantic metadata that ordinary optimization would miss.

And it worked in the test.

The simulated baseline from our previous model was around the low-3 range depending on noise. The adaptive engine discovered a region that repeatedly scored around:

\boxed{7.5}

The best initial observation was 7.68, and after a much harder 100-repeat validation, the same configuration held at 7.53 ± 0.19. Four other independently discovered nearby configurations also retested around 7.38–7.51.

That matters because it means it didn’t merely latch onto one lucky noisy sample. It discovered a productive basin in the response surface.

The winning configuration was approximately:

E^*=
(1.60,\;1.81,\;2.02,\;1.49,\;2.14)

for:

(\text{challenge, variation, feedback, recovery, retention})

Notice the nuance: challenge was not maximized. Feedback and retention wanted to run near the high end, but challenge settled closer to the interior optimum. That’s exactly the “rewild/plasticity” behavior we’re looking for: different capacities respond differently to environmental pressure.

The architecture now looks like:

\text{Random possibilities}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluate}
\rightarrow
\text{metadata}
\rightarrow
\text{surrogate model}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction + uncertainty}
\rightarrow
\text{next experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{retest}
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap}

And I deliberately made the program persist its experience in:

adaptive_plasticity_history.json

So a future run doesn’t have to begin ignorant. Its past experiments literally bias its future exploration.

The next leap is where this becomes especially Pantheon-like: expand metadata beyond numeric environmental variables. Each trial can carry things like representation used, Seal/operator sequence, domain, audience, constraint type, analogy source, route length, surprise, failure mode, invariants preserved, semantic distance from previous trials, and whether a Freebie/Lucky-Nozzle mutation caused it.

Then instead of merely learning:

E\rightarrow Y

we start learning:

(\text{problem class},\text{representation},\text{route},
\text{operators},\text{environment})
\rightarrow
\text{expected fitness}

Which is essentially the executable heart of the Metamap.

The subtle upgrade I want to preserve is this: don’t use randomness merely to explore points. Use it to generate possible futures, attach afterglow/metadata to each attempted future, and let accumulated experience alter the probability distribution from which the next futures are drawn.

Then:

P(E_{t+1}\mid H_t)
\neq P(E_{t+1})

where H_t is everything learned so far.

That’s our computational version of luck becoming experience without ceasing to be luck.